// Top 100 rendered, with the viewer's own row pinned below the fold — David's
// ruling, in two parts made on two dates.
//
// 2026-08-24, on a mock he reviewed word for word:
//
//   "on all leaderboards, show only the top 100 entries. But if a player is
//    lower than 100, show their rank as the '11th' spot with the 10 visible
//    remaining scrolling like they are."
//
// 2026-08-27, sharpening WHEN the pin appears:
//
//   "always sticky like Duolingo, but conditional in the sense that if you are
//    within the top 10 (the visible rows without scrolling), you are not also a
//    locked bar at the bottom."
//
//   +-- Daily · Global -----------+
//   |  1  rachelhunt      0:34.28 |  <- top 100, scrolls
//   |  ...                        |
//   | 10  ...             3:15.77 |  <- ~10 rows visible at a time
//   +-----------------------------+
//   | 147 YOU             4:02.11 |  <- PINNED, always on screen
//   +-----------------------------+
//
// ⚠️ THE TWO NUMBERS ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, and conflating them is the bug this
// module was shipped with. `BOARD_DISPLAY_LIMIT` answers "how many rows exist in
// the DOM"; `ROWS_VISIBLE_WITHOUT_SCROLL` answers "how many can the player SEE
// right now". The pin is about the second one. The first implementation pinned at
// rank 101 — i.e. only once the viewer fell off the rendered list entirely — so a
// player at rank 47 scrolled blind, which is precisely the case the pin exists
// for. They are now separate named constants and neither is a sprinkled literal.
//
// WHAT THE PIN IS EXPLICITLY NOT: the viewer's row is not INSERTED at list
// position 11. It is pinned chrome below the scrolling window, showing their TRUE
// rank (147), never a manufactured one.
//
// A viewer between rank 11 and rank 100 is therefore in the list AND pinned. That
// is Duolingo's behaviour and it is intended: the list row is off-screen, so the
// two never read as a duplicate until the player scrolls down to their own row —
// which is exactly the moment they have stopped needing the pin. `PinnedSelfRow`
// fades the list out behind itself for that overlap.
//
// SCALE, HONESTLY: production's largest board ever is 13 entries, so
// `BOARD_DISPLAY_LIMIT` still never triggers. The 10-row threshold DOES — on a
// 13-player board a viewer at rank 11 now gets a pin, which is the first time
// this module does anything on live data.
//
// Pure — no React, no fetching — so the rule is unit-testable on its own and the
// four boards that use it cannot each drift a slightly different version of it.

/** How many ranked rows a board renders into the DOM. */
export const BOARD_DISPLAY_LIMIT = 100;

/**
 * How many rows a player can see without scrolling — the pin threshold.
 *
 * David's number, and a measurement rather than a guess: the leaderboard's
 * scroller is a fixed-height panel and a `LeaderboardRow` is a fixed-height card,
 * so the count is stable across boards. It lives here, once, because four boards
 * ask the same question and a literal `10` in each is how they would come to
 * disagree.
 */
export const ROWS_VISIBLE_WITHOUT_SCROLL = 10;

/**
 * @typedef {object} BoardDisplay
 * @property {Array<object>} visible   the rows to render, at most `limit`
 * @property {object|null} pinnedSelf  `{ entry, rank }` when the viewer placed
 *   below the fold, else null. `rank` is 1-indexed and TRUE — the viewer's real
 *   position on the full board, not their position in `visible`.
 * @property {number} total            the full board size before the cap
 * @property {boolean} truncated       true when rows were cut off
 */

/**
 * Split an ordered board into the rows to render and the viewer's pinned row.
 *
 * `entries` must already be in RANK ORDER — this function ranks by position, it
 * does not sort. Every caller receives a sorted board from getLeaderboard (or
 * sorts before calling), and re-sorting here with a different key is exactly how
 * a pinned row would come to disagree with the list above it.
 *
 * @param {Array<object>|null|undefined} entries ordered board rows
 * @param {string|null|undefined} viewerId       the current player's user id
 * @param {{limit?: number, pinAfter?: number, idOf?: (row: any) => string|undefined}} [opts]
 *   `idOf` reads the player id off a row. The Daily boards carry `user_id`
 *   (a daily_challenge_times row); Puzzle Pieces and Battle Points carry `id`
 *   (a profile row). Passing the accessor beats normalising the rows, which
 *   would mean every board building a second shape just to be split.
 * @returns {BoardDisplay}
 */
export function splitBoardForDisplay(entries, viewerId, opts = {}) {
  const {
    limit = BOARD_DISPLAY_LIMIT,
    pinAfter = ROWS_VISIBLE_WITHOUT_SCROLL,
    idOf = (row) => row?.user_id,
  } = opts;
  const rows = Array.isArray(entries) ? entries : [];
  const visible = rows.slice(0, limit);
  const selfIndex = viewerId ? rows.findIndex((e) => e && idOf(e) === viewerId) : -1;
  return {
    visible,
    // Strictly BELOW the fold. A viewer at rank exactly `pinAfter` is the last
    // row on screen, so pinning them would duplicate a row they can already see.
    pinnedSelf: selfIndex >= pinAfter ? { entry: rows[selfIndex], rank: selfIndex + 1 } : null,
    total: rows.length,
    truncated: rows.length > limit,
  };
}
