// solveVariant — the IDENTITY fingerprint of a finished solve.
//
// Two players can write the byte-identical equation string and still not have
// made the same solve. The board's seven dice are rolled independently, so the
// same value routinely appears on a white die AND on a power die; when it does,
// "3^3+5*2" no longer says which physical 3 went where. One player may have
// spent the red 3 as the exponent (the power die used correctly, gold ring) and
// the other as the base — same characters, different solve, different score.
//
// Everything downstream of a stored row already knows this: `usedDiceIndices`
// records WHICH board slot each digit came from and `diceColors` records what
// colour sat in that slot, and `getPowerDiceCorrectness` reads both. It is only
// when solves are COMPARED to each other that the identity gets dropped and the
// string is treated as the whole truth. This module is the missing key.
//
// Positional contract (ADR-0002): the Nth digit in equation order is the die at
// `diceColors[usedDiceIndices[N]]`. Indices are per-player — the board can be
// shuffled (see game-flow/shuffleBoard.js) — so a raw index list is NOT
// comparable across players. The COLOUR sequence is, and colour is exactly what
// the renderer and the power-dice rule consume, so two solves sharing a string
// and a colour sequence are interchangeable in every way the app can observe.

/**
 * The colour of each die in EQUATION ORDER, as a comparable string.
 *
 * Returns `''` for a row with no usable dice metadata (legacy rows predate the
 * columns). Those cannot be told apart, so they compare equal to each other and
 * unequal to any row we can actually read — which is the honest answer.
 *
 * @param {number[]|null|undefined} usedDiceIndices - equation order → board slot
 * @param {Array<string>|null|undefined} diceColors - board slot → colour
 * @returns {string} e.g. `'white,red,white,white'`
 */
export const solveColorSignature = (usedDiceIndices, diceColors) => {
  if (!Array.isArray(usedDiceIndices) || !Array.isArray(diceColors)) return '';
  return usedDiceIndices.map((slot) => diceColors[slot] || 'white').join(',');
};
